Intuition
The key observation is that every element in a row is generated from the previous row:

0 generates 01.
1 generates 10.
Instead of generating the entire row, we can recursively find the parent of the kth element in the previous row. The parent is at position (k + 1) / 2.

Approach
If k == 1, return 0, because the first element of every row is always 0.
Find the parent element recursively using kthGrammar(n - 1, (k + 1) / 2).
Determine the current element based on its parent:
If the parent is 0, the odd position contains 0 and the even position contains 1.
If the parent is 1, the odd position contains 1 and the even position contains 0.
Return the resulting element.
Complexity
Time complexity: O(n), because each recursive call reduces n by one.
Space complexity: O(n), due to the recursive call stack.
Code
class Solution {
    public int kthGrammar(int n, int k) {

        if (k == 1) {
            return 0;
        }

        int parent = kthGrammar(n - 1, (k + 1) / 2);

        if (parent == 0) {
            if (k % 2 == 1) {
                return 0;
            } else {
                return 1;
            }
        } else {
            if (k % 2 == 1) {
                return 1;
            } else {
                return 0;
            }
        }
    }
}
